2 gets (n-2,2) and (n-1,2). Then S1 is horizontal (slope 0), S2 is horizontal (slope 0). Not allowed.\n\nCase 2: S1 gets (n-2,1) and (n-1,2); S2 gets (n-2,2) and (n-1,1). Then S1 slope = (2-1)/(n-1 - (n-2)) = 1 (sunny). S2 slope = (1-2)/(n-1 - (n-2)) = -1 (not allowed). So fails.\n\nThus no assignment works. So covering the points (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-1,1),(n-1,2) with two sunny lines is impossible. Therefore, after using a diagonal line to cover (n-2,3) (or any other point), we still have these four points left, which cannot be covered by two sunny lines. So we need to cover at least one of those four points with a non-sunny line (vertical/horizontal/diagonal). But we have already used all non-sunny lines? Let's see.\n\nWe have n-2 non-sunny lines total. We used vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (n-3 vertical lines). We used one diagonal line D (x+y=n+1) covering (n-2,3),(n-1,2),(n,1). That's n-2 non-sunny lines used (n-3 + 1 = n-2). So we have no non-sunny lines left to cover any of the four points (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-1,1),(n-1,2). So we cannot cover them with sunny lines only. So this construction fails.\n\nThus we need to allocate non-sunny lines differently: perhaps use a horizontal line to cover one of those points, reducing the number of points left for sunny lines.\n\nLet's try: Use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (n-3 vertical lines). Use a horizontal line y=1 (non-sunny) covering (n-2,1),(n-1,1),(n,1). Use a diagonal line x+y=n+1 (non-sunny) covering (n-2,3),(n-1,2),(n,1). That's total non-sunny lines: n-3 + 2 = n-1, too many. So we need to reduce vertical lines to n-4. Let's try vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-4 (n-4 vertical lines). Use horizontal line y=1 (covers (n-3,1),(n-2,1),(n-1,1),(n,1)). Use diagonal line x+y=n+1 (covers (n-3,4),(n-2,3),(n-1,2),(n,1)). That's total non-sunny lines: n-4 + 2 = n-2, good. Then we have two sunny lines left.\n\nNow uncovered points after covering columns 1,...,n-4 (vertical), H, D:\n\n- Column n-3: points (n-3